INT. TINY TEA SHOP - MORNING

A shop the width of a hallway. Shelves of tins to the ceiling. A brass scale. A counter polished by decades of elbows. A bell above the door.

PERPETUA OLDFIELD, 60s, in a lilac cardigan, stands behind the counter arranging tins by color. SAMUEL, 30s, a young man in an ill-fitting blazer, stands at the other side with a clipboard.

SAMUEL
Mrs. Oldfield, I'm from the council.

PERPETUA
Lovely. Tea?

SAMUEL
I'm not supposed to.

PERPETUA
Of course. Which kind?

SAMUEL
Whatever you have.

PERPETUA
I have two hundred and fourteen.

SAMUEL
Whichever is fastest.

PERPETUA
Oh, that would be a gunpowder green. It rolls itself.

She takes a tin down, measures leaves onto the brass scale, adds one, takes one away.

SAMUEL
It's about the inspection.

PERPETUA
Mm.

SAMUEL
Your sign is two inches over the permitted size.

PERPETUA
Is it?

SAMUEL
By code.

PERPETUA
Which code?

SAMUEL
Section nineteen. Paragraph four. Subsection... B.

PERPETUA
Subsection B has always been my favorite.

She pours hot water from a kettle into a small white pot. The steam rises between them. Samuel adjusts his tie.

SAMUEL
I don't want to give you a violation.

PERPETUA
Then don't.

SAMUEL
It's the law.

PERPETUA
So it is.

She sets a cup in front of him. He looks at it. He looks at his clipboard.

SAMUEL
I really shouldn't.

PERPETUA
It's only tea.

He takes the cup. Sips. His eyes widen slightly. He lowers the clipboard onto the counter.

SAMUEL
This is very good.

PERPETUA
It's the leaf.

SAMUEL
Is it the sign?

PERPETUA
Excuse me?

SAMUEL
The sign. Is it good tea because of the sign?

PERPETUA
(smiling)
Mr. Samuel. I think you should take another sip.

He does. He sits on the stool by the counter. She goes back to arranging tins. The bell does not ring. Outside, the sign, two inches too large, swings gently in the wind.
